9.3  GRUB vs systemd-boot vs UKI

TL;DR
  - GRUB is the classic bootloader: menus, many filesystems, BIOS and UEFI. Its
    configuration is generated (grub.cfg).
  - systemd-boot is a small UEFI-only menu that reads simple entry files from
    the EFI partition; bootctl manages it.
  - A UKI (unified kernel image) bundles kernel, initramfs and command line into
    one .efi file, which UEFI or systemd-boot starts directly.

      bootctl status
      efibootmgr -v
      ls /boot/efi/EFI

On your machine
  Firmware  UEFI · Secure Boot on
  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64
  Cmdline   BOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.12.48-amd64
            root=UUID=3f1c2a9e-8b7d-4e21-9c55-0a6b2d7e9f10 ro rootflags=subvol=@
            quiet splash
  Init      systemd · default target graphical.target
  Loader    systemd-boot 256.4 · 1 unified kernel image(s)
  Up        3 days, 4 h

Pitfalls
  - Never edit grub.cfg by hand: it is regenerated (see 9.2).
  - systemd-boot needs the kernels on a partition UEFI can read, usually the EFI
    partition: it must be big enough for several kernels.
  - With a UKI, the command line is part of the signed image: changing it means
    building the image again.
  - In dual boot, a Windows update may change the boot order: fix it in the
    firmware menu or with sudo efibootmgr -o.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 9.3 --why

Related
  Commands  bootctl, efibootmgr
  Paths     /boot/grub, /boot/efi/EFI
  Hub       idk boot Boot
